    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/13/intent/source_solution",
      "before": "Cube bases (a+6,1),(y-2,3),(x+3,4),(x,x-y) give first factors a+5,y+1,x-1,2x-y. Expanding their quadratic factors gives a^2+13a+43,y^2-7y+19,x^2+10x+37,x^2-xy+y^2.",
      "after": "Cube bases (a+6,1),(y-2,3),(x+3,4),(x,x-y) give first factors a+5,y+1,x-1,2x-y. Expanding their quadratic factors gives a^2+13a+43,y^2-7y+19,x^2+10x+37,x^2-xy+y^2. For III specifically, ((x+3)-4)((x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16)=(x-1)(x^2+6x+9+4x+12+16)=(x-1)(x^2+10x+37)."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/13/intent/unit_reason",
      "before": "The complete list tests treating an entire expression as a cube base and then simplifying its trinomial, rather than separate substitution clicks.",
      "after": "The complete list tests treating an entire expression as a cube base and then simplifying its trinomial, rather than separate substitution clicks. Row III now has competing first-factor and square-cross-term results to judge. B has the same correct I, II and IV rows as A and differs only in III, so those other rows cannot eliminate it. Whole-list selection still does not certify independently produced working or separate mastery of every subpart."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/13/key/rationales/1/derivation",
      "before": "Row I's binomial is (a+6)-1=a+5, not a+7.",
      "after": "I, II and IV now match the correct complete option. Only III is false: its first factor must be (x+3)-4=x-1, not x+7. At x=0 the candidate gives 259 instead of the original -37."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/13/key/rationales/1/misconception",
      "before": "Add 1 to the composed base despite the original difference of cubes.",
      "after": "For III, use (x+3)+4=x+7 as the first factor while leaving (x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16=x^2+10x+37 unchanged."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/13/key/rationales/2/derivation",
      "before": "Row II needs (y-2)^2-3(y-2)+9=y^2-7y+19.",
      "after": "Row II needs (y-2)^2-3(y-2)+9=y^2-7y+19. Row III needs (x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16=x^2+10x+37; omitting +6x gives x^2+4x+37. At x=2 the candidate product is 49 instead of the original 61."
    },
    {
      "json_pointer": "/questions/13/key/rationales/2/misconception",
      "before": "Expand (y-2)^2 as y^2+4, omitting -4y, then retain -3y+6+9 to get y^2-3y+19.",
      "after": "Expand (y-2)^2 as y^2+4, omitting -4y, then retain -3y+6+9 to get y^2-3y+19. For III, apply the same missing-cross-term procedure: (x+3)^2 is falsely x^2+9, and adding 4x+12+16 yields x^2+4x+37 with first factor x-1 unchanged."
    },
